---
/**
 * foodcraft / HeaderBL — THE STICKY CREAM RAIL. The design's front gate,
 * reproduced: a 93%-opaque cream bar over a 14px backdrop blur, one --line
 * hairline under it, and a single flex row at 17px top and bottom carrying
 * three things — the 26px Fraunces wordmark with its rust town tag on the
 * baseline beside it, five 13px wayfinding links at a 28px gap, and the rust
 * pill (the design's btnNav: the same pill as btnLg at 13px/24px instead of
 * 16px/32px).
 *
 * ROLE: wayfinding, plus the one action this page exists for. The pill is a
 * SIBLING of the link row, never a child of it: the link row becomes a
 * display:none drawer at <=900px and the contact affordance must not leave
 * with it.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — every visible string on this rail, and nothing else:
 *   footer.brand                     — the wordmark (also the footer's; one
 *                                      field, two places, on purpose)
 *   footer.locality                  — the rust town tag beside it
 *   navbar_links.${i}.label          — one nav link's label
 *   navbar_links.${i}.href           — that link's target (data-href-field)
 *   navCtaText                       — the rust pill's label
 *   navCtaHref                       — the pill's target (data-href-field)
 * All six are declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts under the
 * `header` group, so every one is a hook a sidebar input actually owns. A
 * data-field on any other path would be a dead hook nothing can edit.
 *
 * DERIVED, never authored here:
 *   - the wordmark falls back to layout.businessName when content.footer.brand
 *     is blank, because that is the same name from the same submission.
 *   - every in-page href is resolved by onPage() against the ids PageBL really
 *     renders (see the anchor guard below).
 *   - `i` is the ORIGINAL, pre-filter index of a nav link, so dropping an
 *     unreachable row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   - THE DESIGN'S OWN COPY. "Kalinaw Coffee", "Cebu", "Menu", "Our story",
 *     "Gallery", "Visit", "FAQ" and "Contact us" are the DESIGNER filling in
 *     their own template. Not one of those words is in this file. A café whose
 *     owner supplied no nav links gets no nav links, not the designer's five.
 *   - THE WHOLE RAIL, when brand, locality, links and pill are all empty. A
 *     sticky bar with nothing in it is ~78px of dead chrome on every screen,
 *     and it is the BODY content that is counted here — never a tag or a
 *     headline, because lib/derive-content-defaults.ts fills those on every
 *     site and a gate that counted them would always pass.
 *   - the burger, unless there is a drawer for it to open.
 *   - the wordmark, with no name. 'Your Business' once shipped as a real
 *     customer's own wordmark; with nothing supplied the town tag carries the
 *     lock alone, and with neither the lock is gone.
 *   - a nav link with no label, or whose href reaches no section on this page.
 *     A 0-width tap target and a link that scrolls nowhere are both broken.
 *   Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so a blank value removes the ELEMENT and
 *   not just its text — no empty pill, no lock with nothing in it.
 *   There is no star row, rating or numeral on this rail, so nothing here can
 *   print a claim the owner did not make.
 *
 * DECISIONS A READER MIGHT OTHERWISE READ AS OVERSIGHTS
 *
 * 1. WHY THE TINT IS A PLAIN rgba() AND NOT A PSEUDO-ELEMENT. The design's rail
 *    is rgba(255,252,246,.93) — that is --paper at 93%. HeaderBK had to paint
 *    its tint on a ::before because PageBK publishes no --night-rgb; PageBL
 *    DOES publish --paper-rgb (and buildOverrideCss remaps it with the rest of
 *    the palette), so the design's exact value is expressible in one token
 *    declaration. No literal, no extra stacking context, same pixels.
 *
 * 2. WHY THE PILL HOOKS navCtaText AND NOT hero.cta1.text.
 *    components/editor/editorBridge.ts resolves a Blocks toggle to a section by
 *    counting hooks of that prefix, so a header carrying hero.* hooks vanishes
 *    when the owner toggles the Hero block off. navCtaText/navCtaHref belong to
 *    the Header group, so this rail answers only to itself. footer.brand is
 *    safe twice over: the bridge only ever hides `closest('section,footer')`
 *    and a <header> is neither, and the real footer outweighs this file's two
 *    footer.* hooks many times over. The pill does NOT fall back to the hero's
 *    label for its DISPLAY either — showing a string the Header-CTA box does
 *    not contain means the sidebar and the page disagree about what the button
 *    says. An empty box renders an empty rail, not the hero's words.
 *
 * 3. WHY THE NAV-LINK HOVER IS --accent-deep. It is the design's OWN hover: its
 *    stylesheet declares a:hover{color:#8E3F14}, which is --accent-deep. In the
 *    demo that rule is shadowed by the inline colour on each nav link, so the
 *    designer's own page happens to render no hover change — an artefact of
 *    inline-style specificity, not a decision. Shipping a five-link row with no
 *    hover state at all would be an interaction regression dressed up as
 *    fidelity, so the design's stated hover colour is honoured (7.15:1 on
 *    cream, and it rides the same theme axis as every other rust element).
 *
 * 4. WHY THE TOWN TAG IS --accent. The design puts it in rust, as it does every
 *    eyebrow on the page (5.31:1 on cream). --accent is the one token PageBL
 *    hands to the theme engine — the design's own data-props exposes `accent`
 *    as a colour picker seeded #B4541F, so that axis is the DESIGNER'S, not an
 *    invention. Re-deriving it here alone would leave the header rust-less on a
 *    page still full of rust.
 *
 * 5. WHY THE PILL IS 44px AND THE RAIL IS ~78px, NOT THE DESIGN'S ~74px. btnNav
 *    declares no height and lands about 40px tall — under the 44px target size.
 *    Left alone it would be worse in the other direction: the wrapper's .btn
 *    sets min-height:48px and no line-height, so it would inherit body's 1.7,
 *    compute 48.4px and give an 82px rail. So the pill restates the design's
 *    13px/24px padding, takes line-height:1.2 (the `normal` the design actually
 *    renders at) and a 44px floor — the closest height to the design that is
 *    still a legal tap target. The wrapper's scroll-padding-top:88px clears the
 *    result, so in-page anchors still land below the rail.
 *
 * 6. WHY flex:none BECAME flex:0 1 auto ON THE LOCK. The design says flex:none,
 *    which at 360px lets a long single-word café name shove the pill off
 *    screen. The lock shrinks and the wordmark ellipsises instead; at desktop
 *    widths the rendering is identical to the design.
 *
 * 7. THE BURGER AND DRAWER ARE AN ADDITION, NOT A RE-DERIVATION. The design is
 *    a desktop artboard and specifies no mobile rail at all. Five links at
 *    380px would push the page sideways, so the row collapses into a drawer at
 *    one width (<=900px) — the row leaves and the burger arrives together, so
 *    there is never a band where neither is on screen. Nothing in the desktop
 *    rendering moves.
 *
 * 8. target="_blank" ONLY FOR http(s). The design's CTA opens a messenger in a
 *    new tab. An owner may point this at #location or a tel: instead, and
 *    hijacking those would be wrong, so the attribute pair is computed, never
 *    literal — and it is ONE conditional per attribute, so no attribute is ever
 *    emitted twice.
 *
 * 9. NO aria-label ON THE WORDMARK LOCK. Its accessible name is its own text:
 *    the owner's name and the owner's town. Appending a literal like "home"
 *    would announce a word this file never received. The two invisible strings
 *    that ARE written here — "Primary" on the nav landmark and "Menu" on the
 *    icon-only burger — are a11y affordances in the same class as the wrapper's
 *    skip link, never page copy. The burger's three bars are decorative and
 *    carry a literal aria-hidden; nothing on this rail carries a conditional
 *    one, so no element can end up with the attribute twice.
 *
 * Never visibility-gated in this file: PageBL owns `visibility.headerSection`.
 * Buttons here are .btn + .btn--fill from the wrapper. Not .nav-cta, not
 * .btn-primary, not inside .nav-right — buildOverrideCss pins the label colour
 * on all three with !important, and `footer .btn` too, which is a further
 * reason this rail is a <header>.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;
const lay = (layout as any) || {};
const c = (content as any) || {};

// ── ANCHOR GUARD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes nav links to '#about', '#services',
// '#why', '#work', '#visit' and navCtaHref to '#visit', and lib/astro-builder.ts
// falls back to '#about', '#services', '#gallery', '#contact' — ids most designs
// never render, so those links scrolled nowhere. Resolve every in-page href
// against what PageBL ACTUALLY renders: keep it if the id exists, remap it when
// this design carries the same section under another name, otherwise fall back
// (nav links pass '' and are dropped). Off-page hrefs — tel:, mailto:, http(s)
// and anything else without a leading '#' — pass through untouched.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'location', 'faq', 'main']);
// This design renames four derived targets: the services block IS the menu
// (rooms lands there too), '#about' is the café's own story (host and why are
// the same block here), '#work' is the photo wall, and '#visit' / '#contact'
// both mean the location block — address, map and opening hours. A booking link
// has no checkout to reach on this page, so it lands on <main>.
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'location',
  host: 'about',
  rooms: 'menu',
  services: 'menu',
  visit: 'location',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

// The wordmark. 'Your Business' was a shipped placeholder that rendered as the
// customer's own name — with nothing supplied the mark is omitted instead.
// `||`, never `??`: genericContentSchema gives footer.brand
// fallbackPaths ['business_name'] and ContentFieldsAuto's readWithFallbacks
// treats '' as empty, so a CLEARED brand box still shows the business name in
// the sidebar. With `??` an empty string is a value, and the page would have
// gone wordmark-less while the sidebar displayed a name — exactly the
// page/sidebar disagreement decision 2 above refuses for the pill.
const brand = String(c.footer?.brand || lay.businessName || '').trim();
// The town tag. One field, three consumers (here, the footer wordmark and the
// map caption) and nothing derives it, so on a first build it is simply absent
// rather than guessed out of the address.
const locality = String(c.footer?.locality ?? '').trim();

// Nav links. `i` is the ORIGINAL index so per-link edits stay aimed at the row
// the owner clicked even after unreachable rows are dropped.
const navLinks: Array<{ href: string; label: string; i: number }> =
  (Array.isArray(lay.navLinks) ? lay.navLinks : [])
    .map((l: any, i: number) => ({ label: String(l?.label ?? '').trim(), href: onPage(l?.href, ''), i }))
    .filter((l: { href: string; label: string }) => l.href && l.label);

// The rust pill. Label and target both come from the Header group; an empty
// label removes the whole element rather than leaving a rust rectangle.
const ctaText = String(c.navCtaText ?? '').trim();
// '#location' is the fallback because that is where this design puts the
// address, the map and the opening hours — the page has no checkout to send a
// booking link to.
const ctaHref = onPage(c.navCtaHref, '#location');
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);

// The rail renders only when it CARRIES something — the lock, the links or the
// pill. There is no derived default that can fake all four of these at once.
const hasRail = Boolean(brand || locality || navLinks.length > 0 || ctaText);
---
{hasRail && (
  <header class="headbl">
    <div class="wrap hb-in">
      {(brand || locality) && (
        <a class="hb-lock" href="#main">
          {brand && <span class="hb-mark" data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>}
          {locality && <span class="hb-town" data-field="footer.locality">{locality}</span>}
        </a>
      )}
      {navLinks.length > 0 && (
        <nav class="hb-nav" id="hb-nav-BL" aria-label="Primary">
          {navLinks.map((link) => (
            <a
              href={link.href}
              data-field={`navbar_links.${link.i}.label`}
              data-href-field={`navbar_links.${link.i}.href`}
            >{link.label}</a>
          ))}
        </nav>
      )}
      {/* OUTSIDE .hb-nav on purpose: .hb-nav is a display:none drawer at
          <=900px and the one action this template exists for must survive it. */}
      {ctaText && (
        <a
          href={ctaHref}
          class="btn btn--fill hb-cta"
          target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
          rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
          data-field="navCtaText"
          data-href-field="navCtaHref"
        >{ctaText}</a>
      )}
      {navLinks.length > 0 && (
        <button class="hb-burger" type="button" aria-label="Menu" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="hb-nav-BL">
          <span aria-hidden="true"></span><span aria-hidden="true"></span><span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
      )}
    </div>
  </header>
)}
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    // Null-guarded rather than conditionally rendered: an is:inline script must
    // stay at the top level, and with no rail (or no drawer) it simply exits.
    // 'headbl' is family-unique because a preview harness can render two
    // wrappers into one document and a shared class cross-wires the drawers.
    var rail = document.querySelector('header.headbl');
    if (!rail) return;
    var btn = rail.querySelector('.hb-burger');
    if (!btn) return;
    function setOpen(open) {
      rail.classList.toggle('open', open);
      btn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', open ? 'true' : 'false');
    }
    btn.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(!rail.classList.contains('open')); });
    // '.hb-cta' is in this selector because the pill lives OUTSIDE the drawer:
    // tapping it while the drawer is open must still close the drawer.
    rail.querySelectorAll('.hb-nav a, .hb-cta').forEach(function (a) {
      a.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(false); });
    });
    document.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) { if (e.key === 'Escape') setOpen(false); });
  })();
</script>
<style>
  /* The design: sticky, 93% cream, blur(14px), one hairline under it. The tint
     is a straight rgba() on --paper-rgb, which PageBL publishes and the theme
     engine remaps with the rest of the palette (see note 1 above) — so this is
     the design's own rgba(255,252,246,.93) with no literal in it. */
  .headbl {
    /* z-index is the design's own 50. Nothing else in this template stacks
       above 3, and the wrapper's skip link sits at 100, so the rail clears the
       page and the skip link still clears the rail. */
    position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 50;
    background-color: rgba(var(--paper-rgb), .93);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(14px);
    backdrop-filter: blur(14px);
  }

  /* The design's row: 17px top and bottom, 24px gap, three parts spread apart.
     Its 32px side padding is the .wrap's job in this family (min(1240px,92vw)),
     so only the vertical value is restated here.
     NOT position:relative — the <=900px drawer is absolutely positioned and
     resolves against .headbl so it spans the full rail width, not the wrap.
     justify-content is flex-end rather than the design's space-between because
     the auto margins below already reproduce space-between whenever two or more
     parts are in flow (an auto margin eats the free space before
     justify-content ever sees it). It only takes effect in the degenerate cases
     the design never drew: a café with no brand and no town tag, where at
     <=900px the drawer has left the flow and the pill and burger would
     otherwise sit hard LEFT. */
  .hb-in { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: flex-end; gap: 24px; padding: 17px 0; }

  /* Wordmark + town tag share a baseline, exactly as the design draws them.
     Both the lock and the link row take an auto right margin: with all three
     present that reproduces the design's space-between, and with either one
     missing the pill still sits hard right. */
  .hb-lock {
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 10px;
    margin-right: auto; flex: 0 1 auto; min-width: 0; color: var(--ink);
  }
  .hb-mark {
    font-family: var(--disp); font-weight: 400; font-size: 26px;
    letter-spacing: -.01em; color: var(--ink);
    /* line-height is the DESIGN'S value: it states none, so its wordmark
       renders at `normal`. It is RESTATED rather than left to inherit because
       the wrapper's body sets 1.7 and 26px of Fraunces would sit in a 44px
       line box. It must not be TIGHTENED either: `normal` is the face's own
       ascent+descent, and any smaller line box is clipped by the
       overflow:hidden that the ellipsis below requires — shaving the tail off
       a descender (Bayang, Kape) and the tilde off a capital (Niño). */
    line-height: normal;
    /* A long single-word café name must not push the pill off a 360px screen;
       flex items are blockified, so ellipsis applies here. */
    min-width: 0; overflow: hidden; white-space: nowrap; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }
  .hb-town {
    flex: 0 1 auto; min-width: 0;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 600; font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: .34em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
    overflow: hidden; white-space: nowrap; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }

  .hb-nav { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 28px; margin-right: auto; }
  .hb-nav a {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 400; font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    transition: color .18s ease;
  }
  /* The design's own hover, straight from its stylesheet: #8E3F14, which is
     --accent-deep — see note 3 in the frontmatter. Same theme axis as --accent,
     over a rest colour of --ink-soft at 6.71:1. */
  .hb-nav a:hover { color: var(--accent-deep); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .hb-nav a { transition: none; } }

  /* The design's btnNav is the wrapper's .btn--fill at a smaller size: rust
     fill, --on-accent label, 100px pill, 12px/.15em caps, 13px/24px padding.
     Only the METRICS are restated — no fill, no label colour, no box-shadow —
     so the wrapper's hover darkening and its focus ring both stay intact.
     line-height and min-height are the two values that are not verbatim; note 5
     in the frontmatter says why 44px is the honest number. */
  .hb-cta { flex: none; padding: 13px 24px; line-height: 1.2; min-height: 44px; }

  /* The design specifies no mobile rail (note 7). The burger borrows the
     design's OWN secondary-control treatment — a --card fill inside a
     --line-btn hairline, which is exactly its outlined button — so the addition
     stays inside the template's vocabulary. */
  .hb-burger {
    display: none; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center; gap: 5px;
    width: 46px; height: 44px; padding: 11px 10px; flex: none;
    background-color: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line-btn);
    border-radius: var(--r-pill); cursor: pointer;
  }
  .hb-burger span {
    display: block; width: 100%; height: 2px; background-color: var(--ink);
    transition: transform .2s ease, opacity .2s ease;
  }
  header.headbl.open .hb-burger span:nth-child(1) { transform: translateY(7px) rotate(45deg); }
  header.headbl.open .hb-burger span:nth-child(2) { opacity: 0; }
  header.headbl.open .hb-burger span:nth-child(3) { transform: translateY(-7px) rotate(-45deg); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .hb-burger span { transition: none; } }

  /* ONE collapse width: the row leaves and the burger arrives together, so
     there is never a band where neither is on screen. The drawer is OPAQUE
     --paper-2 rather than the rail's 93% cream: it hangs over live page content
     and a translucent panel would print the page through the links. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .hb-burger { display: flex; }
    .hb-nav {
      position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0; top: 100%;
      display: none; flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; gap: 0;
      background-color: var(--paper-2);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
      box-shadow: 0 14px 30px rgba(var(--ink-rgb), .12);
      padding: 4px 0 10px; max-height: 78vh; overflow-y: auto;
    }
    header.headbl.open .hb-nav { display: flex; }
    .hb-nav a {
      display: flex; align-items: center; min-height: 48px; padding: 12px 22px;
      font-size: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-3);
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .hb-in { gap: 10px; padding: 14px 0; }
    .hb-mark { font-size: 22px; }
    .hb-town { letter-spacing: .24em; }
    /* display:block, not inline-flex, so text-overflow actually applies; a long
       owner-written label is ellipsised rather than widening the rail past the
       viewport. 12px padding over a 20px line box still clears a 44px target. */
    .hb-cta {
      display: block; line-height: 20px; padding: 12px 15px;
      font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: .1em;
      max-width: 42vw; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
    }
  }
  /* At 360px the town tag is the first thing to go: the wordmark, the pill and
     the burger are all load-bearing, the tag is a flourish. */
  @media (max-width: 400px) {
    .hb-town { display: none; }
    .hb-cta { max-width: 38vw; padding: 12px 12px; }
  }
</style>
